Theorem 6.1 states that the open subsets of \(\mathbf{P}\omega\) are exactly the sets \(\{x \mid f(x) \supseteq 0\}\) for continuous \(f\), with the equivalent writing \(0 \in f(x)\), and that \(\{x \mid 0 \in x\}\) is the typical open set, of which every other open is a continuous inverse image. `theorem_6_1` states both directions for Scott-continuous maps: \(\{x \mid 0 \in f(x)\}\) is Scott open, and every Scott-open set equals that set for its open characteristic. The forward half says that \(0 \in f(x)\) means \(0\) lies in the Scott union of \(f\) along \(x\), so some finite \(e_n \subseteq x\) already satisfies \(0 \in f(e_n)\), while the converse inclusion is monotonicity of \(f\). The converse half says the open characteristic is Scott continuous, that \(x\) in the open set supplies a basis element \(e_n \subseteq x\) still in the set and hence \(0\) in the characteristic, and that \(0\) in the characteristic supplies such an \(e_n\), after which the upper-set property returns \(x\) to the set. `typicalG_isScottOpen` applies the first half to the identity, `typicalG_mem_ScottG` records that this typical set lies in the class of Scott-open sets, and `typicalG_universal` is the inverse-image form of the same biconditional. **Theorem:** yes — the \(\mathfrak{G}\) biconditional and the typical-set representation are those four glosses. **Proof:** yes — the finite-basis calculation and the open characteristic prove the biconditional, and the note gives no separate proof.
